# RunicAI RLM Coding Agent

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:kino, "~> 0.19"},
  {:runic_ai, path: "~/wrk/runic_ai"}
])

Code.require_file("support/runic_ai_livebook.exs", __DIR__)
```

## What This Notebook Teaches

`RunicAI.Coding.RLM.Agent` is the strategic coding agent. It does not hide a
large prompt loop. It treats planning as data, validates that data, compiles it
into a Runic workflow, and then executes bounded nodes.

It is good at:

* decomposing a coding objective into a visible workflow graph
* keeping codebase sources outside the root prompt until needed
* validating generated workflow plans before they can mutate anything
* mixing tools, deterministic steps, child standard agents, edits, review gates,
  verification gates, reducers, and workflow patches
* recording episodes, artifacts, checkpoints, scorecards, and repair needs

Use the standard coding agent for direct local work. Use the RLM coding agent
when you want the agent to first design the workflow that will do the work.

## Optional Provider Setup

The main cells use deterministic proposals and fake model responses. Use this
only for the optional live-model proposal cell.

```elixir
setup = RunicAI.Livebook.Setup.inputs()
```

## Build A Demo Workspace

```elixir
alias RunicAI.Coding.Workspace
alias RunicAI.Coding.RLM.Agent, as: RLMAgent
alias RunicAI.Coding.RLM.{
  CodebaseEnvironment,
  Coordinator,
  ProposalNode,
  RecipeLibrary,
  WorkflowProposal,
  WorkflowValidator
}

root = RunicAI.Livebook.CodingDemo.sample_project!("rlm-coding-agent")
workspace = Workspace.new!(root: root)
objective = "Where is Demo.answer/0?"

%{workspace_root: workspace.root, objective: objective}
```

## Architecture

The RLM agent has two important workflow shapes:

* the planned workflow, which is the inert `WorkflowProposal` and normalized
  `RunicAI.RLM.Plan`
* the resulting Runic workflow, which is compiled from the accepted proposal and
  executes each node

```elixir
Kino.Mermaid.new("""
flowchart TD
  objective["Coding objective"] --> prepare["Prepare CodebaseEnvironment"]
  prepare --> sources["SourceIndex + external RLM refs"]
  sources --> coordinator["Coordinator proposes WorkflowProposal"]
  coordinator --> validator["WorkflowValidator policy checks"]
  validator -->|accepted| plan["WorkflowProposal.to_plan/1"]
  validator -->|accepted| compiler["WorkflowCompiler.compile/3"]
  compiler --> runic["Executable Runic workflow"]
  runic --> nodes["Execute nodes in topological order"]
  nodes --> episodes["RLM episodes + trace"]
  episodes --> progress["ProgressGate and scorecards"]
  progress -->|satisfied| output["Final output"]
  progress -->|needs repair| repair["RepairLoop"]
  repair --> nodes
  validator -->|rejected| fallback["Fallback recipe or explicit error"]
""")
```

## Prepare The Codebase Environment

`CodebaseEnvironment` registers the repository manifest, `AGENTS.md`, and files
as RLM sources. The proposal and workflow carry refs and summaries instead of
blindly pasting the whole workspace into a prompt.

```elixir
{:ok, environment} = RLMAgent.prepare(workspace, index?: true, file_limit: 40)

Kino.DataTable.new(RunicAI.Livebook.CodingDemo.source_rows(environment))
```

```elixir
{:ok, artifact} = CodebaseEnvironment.materialize(environment, "lib/demo.ex")
source_ref = List.first(artifact.refs)

%{
  artifact_id: artifact.id,
  source_id: source_ref && source_ref.source_id,
  preview: String.slice(artifact.content, 0, 120)
}
```

## The Proposal Contract

A proposal is inert data. It can be model-generated, recipe-seeded, or provided
directly by your application. It cannot execute until validation accepts it.

```elixir
Kino.DataTable.new([
  %{part: "WorkflowProposal", purpose: "Objective, sources, nodes, edges, policies, stop conditions"},
  %{part: "ProposalNode", purpose: "A model-proposable node with id, kind, params, policy, provenance"},
  %{part: "WorkflowValidator", purpose: "Policy checks for graph shape, tools, commands, edits, scripts, budgets"},
  %{part: "WorkflowCompiler", purpose: "Turns accepted proposal nodes into executable Runic steps"},
  %{part: "Episode", purpose: "Per-node status, output, artifacts, worker id, and provenance"}
])
```

```elixir
Kino.DataTable.new(
  Enum.map(ProposalNode.allowed_kinds(), fn kind ->
    %{
      kind: inspect(kind),
      meaning:
        case kind do
          :tool -> "Call a coding tool from the registry"
          :agent -> "Call the standard coding agent as a child workflow"
          :edit -> "Apply a validated edit patch set"
          :verification_gate -> "Evaluate deterministic acceptance checks"
          :reducer -> "Combine parent outputs into a report, findings, plan, or verification summary"
          :review_gate -> "Record a review boundary in the generated graph"
          :workflow_patch -> "Validate a bounded recursive plan patch"
          :llm_leaf -> "Run one bounded model leaf"
          :command -> "Run an allowed argv command when policy enables commands"
          :script -> "Run an opt-in script node"
          :deterministic_step -> "Run fixed local logic such as source_summary"
        end
    }
  end)
)
```

## Planned Workflow From A Recipe Seed

The coordinator can use a recipe seed for high-confidence routes. This
read-only repo-QA plan discovers files, reads the relevant files, and reduces
the result into an answer.

```elixir
{:ok, proposal, validation_report} =
  RLMAgent.propose(objective, environment,
    command: :repo_qa,
    validation_opts: [allowed_tools: ["find_files", "read_many"]]
  )

{:ok, plan} = WorkflowProposal.to_plan(proposal)

Kino.Layout.tabs(
  "Proposal graph": Kino.Mermaid.new(RunicAI.Livebook.CodingDemo.proposal_mermaid(proposal)),
  "Proposal nodes": Kino.DataTable.new(RunicAI.Livebook.CodingDemo.proposal_rows(proposal)),
  "Validation report": Kino.Tree.new(validation_report)
)
```

## The Consequence RLM Plan

`WorkflowProposal.to_plan/1` maps coding-specific node kinds into the generic
RLM plan vocabulary. This is useful when you want to inspect the recursive plan
shape independently from the compiled Runic workflow.

```elixir
Kino.Layout.tabs(
  "RLM plan graph": Kino.Mermaid.new(RunicAI.Livebook.CodingDemo.plan_mermaid(plan)),
  "RLM plan nodes": Kino.DataTable.new(RunicAI.Livebook.CodingDemo.plan_rows(plan)),
  Plan: Kino.Tree.new(plan)
)
```

## The Resulting Executable Workflow

The compiler turns accepted nodes into named Runic steps and wires them by the
proposal edges. This is the workflow that actually executes.

```elixir
{:ok, compiled} =
  RLMAgent.compile(proposal, environment,
    validation_opts: [allowed_tools: ["find_files", "read_many"]]
  )

RunicAI.Livebook.WorkflowViz.tabs(compiled.workflow)
```

## Execute The Repo-QA Workflow

This run is fully deterministic. No model is needed because the recipe proposal
uses coding tools and a deterministic reducer.

```elixir
{:ok, repo_qa_result} =
  RLMAgent.run(workspace, objective,
    command: :repo_qa,
    validation_opts: [allowed_tools: ["find_files", "read_many"]]
  )

repo_qa_result.output
```

```elixir
Kino.Layout.tabs(
  Episodes: Kino.DataTable.new(RunicAI.Livebook.CodingDemo.episode_rows(repo_qa_result.episodes)),
  Trace: Kino.Tree.new(repo_qa_result.trace),
  Context: Kino.Tree.new(repo_qa_result.context),
  "Compiled workflow": Kino.Tree.new(repo_qa_result.compiled)
)
```

## Tool, HITL, And Subagent Boundaries

Every node kind has a different authority boundary. Tool and command nodes go
through registries and policies. Agent nodes call the standard coding agent and
return a `ChildResult`. Verification gates check objective evidence. Review
gates make review boundaries visible in the generated graph.

```elixir
Kino.Mermaid.new("""
flowchart LR
  proposal["Accepted proposal node"] --> kind{"kind"}
  kind -->|tool| registry["Tool registry"]
  registry --> tool["Tool.Definition.execute"]
  tool --> observation["Tool output / artifact"]
  kind -->|agent| child["Standard coding agent child"]
  child --> child_result["ChildResult"]
  kind -->|edit| patch_set["EditPatchSet"]
  patch_set --> patch_result["PatchResult"]
  kind -->|verification_gate| checks["Acceptance checks"]
  checks --> scorecard["WorkflowScorecard"]
  kind -->|review_gate| review["Visible review boundary"]
  observation --> reducer["Reducer"]
  child_result --> reducer
  patch_result --> reducer
  scorecard --> reducer
""")
```

## Coordinator-Generated Subagent Plan

This cell simulates a model-generated proposal by returning JSON through the LLM
client contract. The proposal calls a standard `read_only` child agent, verifies
that the child completed, and reduces the evidence into a report.

```elixir
generated_object = %{
  "objective" => "Ask a child agent to explain Demo.answer/0",
  "nodes" => [
    %{
      "id" => "inspect",
      "kind" => "agent",
      "params" => %{"mode" => "read_only", "task" => "Explain Demo.answer/0"}
    },
    %{
      "id" => "verify-child",
      "kind" => "verification_gate",
      "params" => %{
        "checks" => [
          %{
            "id" => "inspect-completed",
            "type" => "child_status",
            "params" => %{"child_id" => "inspect", "status" => "completed"}
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    %{"id" => "report", "kind" => "reducer", "params" => %{"reducer" => "report"}}
  ],
  "edges" => [
    %{"from" => "inspect", "to" => "verify-child"},
    %{"from" => "verify-child", "to" => "report"}
  ],
  "stop_conditions" => ["child answer verified and reported"]
}

{:ok, generated_proposal, generated_report} =
  RLMAgent.propose("Ask a child agent to explain Demo.answer/0", environment,
    client: RunicAI.Livebook.CodingDemo.json_object_client(generated_object),
    fallback?: false
  )

{:ok, generated_plan} = WorkflowProposal.to_plan(generated_proposal)

Kino.Layout.tabs(
  "Generated proposal": Kino.Mermaid.new(RunicAI.Livebook.CodingDemo.proposal_mermaid(generated_proposal)),
  "Generated RLM plan": Kino.Mermaid.new(RunicAI.Livebook.CodingDemo.plan_mermaid(generated_plan)),
  Report: Kino.Tree.new(generated_report)
)
```

```elixir
{:ok, child_run} =
  RLMAgent.run(workspace, "Ask a child agent to explain Demo.answer/0",
    proposal: generated_proposal,
    client: RunicAI.Livebook.CodingDemo.text_client("Demo.answer/0 returns 41 from lib/demo.ex.")
  )

Kino.Layout.tabs(
  Output: Kino.Tree.new(child_run.output),
  "Child results": Kino.Tree.new(child_run.context.child_results),
  Episodes: Kino.DataTable.new(RunicAI.Livebook.CodingDemo.episode_rows(child_run.episodes)),
  Workflow: RunicAI.Livebook.WorkflowViz.tabs(child_run.workflow)
)
```

## Deterministic Mutating Plan

Mutating proposals require explicit policy. This example applies one edit and
then proves it with a source assertion. The progress gate is disabled only to
keep this cell fully deterministic and focused on the explicit verification
gate; production mutating runs should keep progress verification enabled or
provide the progress policy your application expects.

```elixir
mutation_root = RunicAI.Livebook.CodingDemo.sample_project!("rlm-coding-agent-mutation")
mutation_workspace = Workspace.new!(root: mutation_root)
mutation_environment = RLMAgent.prepare!(mutation_workspace, index?: true, file_limit: 40)

mutation_proposal =
  WorkflowProposal.new!(
    "Add Demo.extra/0",
    [
      ProposalNode.new!("edit-demo", :edit,
        params: %{
          operations: [
            %{
              kind: "replace_file",
              params: %{
                path: "lib/demo.ex",
                content: "defmodule Demo do\n  def answer, do: 41\n  def extra, do: :ok\nend\n"
              }
            }
          ],
          expected_changed_files: ["lib/demo.ex"]
        }
      ),
      ProposalNode.new!("verify-extra", :verification_gate,
        params: %{
          checks: [
            %{
              id: "extra-source",
              type: "source_contains",
              params: %{path: "lib/demo.ex", text: "def extra"}
            }
          ]
        }
      ),
      ProposalNode.new!("summarize", :reducer, params: %{reducer: "report"})
    ],
    edges: [
      %{from: "edit-demo", to: "verify-extra"},
      %{from: "verify-extra", to: "summarize"}
    ],
    stop_conditions: ["Demo.extra/0 exists in source"]
  )

mutation_validation_opts = [
  allowed_kinds: ProposalNode.allowed_kinds(),
  allow_mutation?: true
]

{:ok, mutation_result} =
  RLMAgent.run(mutation_workspace, "Add Demo.extra/0",
    proposal: mutation_proposal,
    validation_opts: mutation_validation_opts,
    progress_gate?: false
  )

Kino.Layout.tabs(
  "Mutation proposal": Kino.Mermaid.new(RunicAI.Livebook.CodingDemo.proposal_mermaid(mutation_proposal)),
  Output: Kino.Tree.new(mutation_result.output),
  Source: Kino.Text.new(File.read!(Path.join(mutation_root, "lib/demo.ex"))),
  Episodes: Kino.DataTable.new(RunicAI.Livebook.CodingDemo.episode_rows(mutation_result.episodes))
)
```

## Policy Rejection

Generated workflows fail closed. Commands, scripts, edits, missing verification
contracts, cycles, unknown edges, and disallowed tools are rejected unless the
caller explicitly enables the needed policy.

```elixir
unsafe_proposal =
  WorkflowProposal.new!(
    "Try an unsafe command",
    [
      ProposalNode.new!("shell", :command,
        params: %{args: %{argv: ["rm", "-rf", "."]}}
      )
    ],
    stop_conditions: ["command attempted"]
  )

WorkflowValidator.validate(unsafe_proposal, allowed_kinds: ProposalNode.allowed_kinds())
```

## Optional Live-Model Proposal

Run this only after setting `OPENAI_API_KEY` in the setup cell. It asks the
coordinator to generate a proposal, validates it, and renders the planned graph
without executing it.

```elixir
RunicAI.Livebook.Setup.require_openai_key!()

model = RunicAI.Livebook.Setup.read_model!(setup)
llm_opts =
  RunicAI.Livebook.Setup.llm_opts(
    model,
    max_output_tokens: RunicAI.Livebook.Setup.read_max_output_tokens(setup)
  )

{:ok, live_proposal, live_report} =
  RLMAgent.propose("Explain the Demo module and cite files.", environment,
    model: model,
    llm_opts: llm_opts,
    fallback?: false,
    validation_opts: [allowed_tools: ["find_files", "read_many", "read_file", "search_text"]]
  )

Kino.Layout.tabs(
  "Live proposal": Kino.Mermaid.new(RunicAI.Livebook.CodingDemo.proposal_mermaid(live_proposal)),
  "Validation report": Kino.Tree.new(live_report),
  "Proposal data": Kino.Tree.new(live_proposal)
)
```

## How To Leverage It

Start with a recipe proposal for known routes such as `:repo_qa`, `:review`, or
`:verify`. Move to generated proposals when the objective needs dynamic
coordination. Keep validation narrow, require explicit mutation policy for edits
or commands, pass contracts to child agents when completion evidence matters,
and inspect the proposal graph, RLM plan graph, compiled workflow, episodes, and
scorecards before trusting a run in production.
